@@ -884,6 +884,7 @@ func (p *sharedProcessor) distribute(obj interface{}, sync bool) {

 func (p *sharedProcessor) run(ctx context.Context) {
        func() {
+               time.Sleep(time.Second * 3)
                p.listenersLock.RLock()
                defer p.listenersLock.RUnlock()
                for listener := range p.listeners {
```

It fails with "timed out waiting for informer to stop".

Interestingly, if I do the same in a synctest, the `time.Sleep(10)` blocks:
```Go
func TestListenerStartup(t *testing.T) { synctest.Test(t, testListenerStartup) }
func testListenerStartup(t *testing.T) {
   <as above>
````

It then never gets to the `t.Log("stopping")`. I think that's because mutexes are not "durably blocking", so once the deadlock occurs, it just waits forever for the stuck goroutines instead of advancing time and returning from the `Sleep` call.

@pohly thanks for the candid feedback and for the simpler reproduction case. I genuinely appreciate you taking the time to correct my analysis.
I see now where my reasoning on the startup ordering was flawed.
I'll be more rigorous in verifying my assumptions against the test cases next time. Thanks again for the guidance.

