@ffromani  Time series as follow： 
t0: pod1 starts to Allocate gpu and setting `m.devicesToReuse[pod1.uid]=aaa`,and then`allocateContainerResources` call rpc;
t1: pod2 starts to Allocate gpu and setting `m.devicesToReuse[pod2.uid]=bbb`,meanwhile `delete(m.m.devicesToReuse, pod1.uid)`;
t2: pod1 runs to `m.podDevices.removeContainerAllocatedResources` and `m.devicesToReuse[pod1.uid]` already deleted in t1. Those will call panic.

thanks @kkkkun ! now makes sense and everything clicks.

For posterity (including yesterday's myself), this is a rarely seen flow and a nasty interaction which seems to have been around since long time. 

Let's consider the kube codebase at b3f3cb88e8fb1b1b7e5e890a9d70ab21fbbab40a 
So, one would expect `Allocate` to be called in the admission flow, and that's indeed the case. But there's also another **potential** call site from `SyncPod`, with this call tree:
```
podSyncer.SyncPod()
    └── kubeGenericRuntimeManager.SyncPod()
        └── start() (local helper function)
            └── startContainer()
                └── generateContainerConfig()
                    └── runtimeHelper.GenerateRunContainerOptions()
                        └── Kubelet.GenerateRunContainerOptions()
                            └── containerManager.GetResources()
                                └── deviceManager.GetDeviceRunContainerOptions()
```

so we go back in devicemanager.GetDeviceRunContainerOptions()
```
// GetDeviceRunContainerOptions checks whether we have cached containerDevices
// for the passed-in <pod, container> and returns its DeviceRunContainerOptions
// for the found one. An empty struct is returned in case no cached state is found.
func (m *ManagerImpl) GetDeviceRunContainerOptions(ctx context.Context, pod *v1.Pod, container *v1.Container) (*DeviceRunContainerOptions, error) {
	logger := klog.FromContext(ctx)
	podUID := string(pod.UID)
	contName := container.Name
	needsReAllocate := false
	for k, v := range container.Resources.Limits {
		resource := string(k)
		if !m.isDevicePluginResource(resource) || v.Value() == 0 {
			continue
		}
		err := m.callPreStartContainerIfNeeded(ctx, podUID, contName, resource)
		if err != nil {
			return nil, err
		}

		if !m.checkPodActive(pod) {
			logger.V(5).Info("Pod deleted from activePods, skip to reAllocate", "pod", klog.KObj(pod), "podUID", podUID, "containerName", container.Name)
			continue
		}

		// This is a device plugin resource yet we don't have cached
		// resource state. This is likely due to a race during node
		// restart. We re-issue allocate request to cover this race.
		if m.podDevices.containerDevices(podUID, contName, resource) == nil {
			needsReAllocate = true
		}
	}
	if needsReAllocate {
		logger.V(2).Info("Needs to re-allocate device plugin resources for pod", "pod", klog.KObj(pod), "containerName", container.Name)
		if err := m.Allocate(pod, container); err != nil {
			return nil, err
		}
	}
	return m.podDevices.deviceRunContainerOptions(logger, string(pod.UID), container.Name), nil
}
```


GetDeviceRunContainerOptions may call again Allocate, which indeed triggers a race. According to the code comment, we have a fix for a potential race which causes another race, and the fact two races have to interact after a restart justifies why we seen this bug rarely, till the AI wave called for many more GPUs, which seems to made this race more likely, albeit still relatively rare.

Trying to understand why we reallocate so late in the flow in `GetDeviceRunContainerOptions` lead us straight to 2019 and to github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/73824 , which unfortunately is not very rich in background context.
https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/issues/64632 was mentioned, but while this helps partially to understand the context at time, is ilttle help nowadays.

Ideally, we should de-entangle the Allocate from `GetDeviceRunContainerOptions`. We should make the kubelet never allow pods to be admitted whose devices are not Allocated, because is safer in general *and* simplifies our code.
But the very fact we had this issue still now demonstrates we're not there yet.

